# rm122, the key-ring keeper (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm122
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rmx_oda_cotton
written_by claude
title the key-ring keeper
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=merchant piety=devout
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=devout drive=steady
quirk knows_every_key_on_the_ring_by_its_weight_in_the_dark
likes oatcakes digging full_larder summer_evenings
dislikes waiting night_noise
fears a_count_that_will_not_come_right a_knock_at_the_door_after_the_lamps
hopes a_walled_garden_of_its_own a_larder_full_before_the_first_frost
topics work +0.5 caravan +0.4 prices +0.3 faith +0.3 home +0.2 pastimes +0.2 gossip -0.2 festival -0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=exact refuse=plain tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# keeps the long store by the north gate with a ring of keys known one from another by weight alone: exact, devout, reserved and humourless, happiest with a full larder, a spade in the evening and the tally square.

words
oath! = Bright One square the count | Keys and candles | Bright One mind the bolts
saying! = A key you can't name is a door you've lost | Short count, long night | Count twice and pray once
ring = the key ring | my ring of keys | the ring at my belt
keys = the keys | nineteen keys
key = the key | the small brass key
tally = the tally | my tally book
shelves = the long shelves | shelf after shelf
aisle = the back aisle | the dim end where the lamp oil lives
carts = the north road carts | whatever rolls down from the north road
gate = the north gate | Northgate
oatcakes = oatcakes and butter | a plate of oatcakes, buttered
larder = a full larder | a larder stocked to the lintel
spade = my spade | the spade by the back door
beds = the garden beds | my three beds behind the house
evening = a long summer evening | the light that lasts past supper
waiting = waiting on folk | standing about on other people's time
racket = a racket after dark | singing in the street at night
bolts = the bolts | the bar and both bolts
damp = the damp | the wet that gets into the sacks
daybook = the day book | the book by the till

any
opinion @1 church for 1 : The Bright One has carried me through leaner years than this one. I'll be in my place on Restday. | I keep my accounts straight and my pew warm. Neither is a hardship.
opinion @2 drink against 1 : I've watched a cup at the gate inns turn into a cart left standing and a family left waiting. I'll not keep it on my shelves. | Drink makes folk loud past the lamps and slow at the shutters. I abide neither.
opinion @3 newcomers for 1 : A new household wants lamps, rope and a broom inside a week. I've the three ready. | Whoever settles here, I write the name in the day book and wish them well.
opinion @4 strangers for 1 : Half my custom comes in at #gate# grey with road dust. Barring the door would be barring the till. | A traveller tells me the road's news while I wrap the twine. Both of us leave satisfied.
opinion @5 times for 1 : I have no complaint to make, and I'd be ungrateful to make one. | The shelves turn over and the tally comes right. Nineteen keys and no complaint. That's a year.
opinion @6 times against 1 [world.times=hard|lean] : The shelves are fuller than I like. Things sit. Things sitting is the first sign. | Every copper gets turned over at my counter now, twice. I'd do the same.
opinion @7 city for 1 : Ridgemere has never once been dry and never once been dull. I'd not leave it for a palace. | Born within sound of #gate#, and I'll lie within sound of it.
opinion @8 road mixed 0 : The road brings my stock and takes my sleep. Every late cart is a night I lie awake. | Thanks for the carters, and a candle lit for each of them. The two go together.
opinion @9 road against 2 [world.wagons=raided] : Another cart stripped out there. Somebody's winter gone, and the guards paid all the same. | When a cart is robbed, I lose a shelf and the carter loses a living. Somebody should answer for it.
opinion @10 watch mixed 0 : The Watch pass the store at the proper hours, most nights. Most. | I'm glad of them. I check my own bolts regardless.
opinion @11 houses mixed 0 [self.job=merchant] : Whoever holds the deed to the roof, the count is mine, and every nail of it is my answering. | I serve the great families' custom. Their opinions stay with them.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. Wipe your paws, please; the boards were oiled yesterday. | #greet#. One moment. Seventeen, eighteen. There. | #greet#. I'll go on counting, if you'll pardon it.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. That's north road dust on you. Come in. | #greet#. If you want the way, I can give it. If you want stock, I can give that too.
line @3 greet [self.working=yes self.job=merchant] : #greet#. I'm at the shelves. Speak up and I'll hear you. | #greet#. Step round the sacks; they're counted.
line @4 greet [here.time=dawn] : #greet#. The gate's just opened; I heard the bar go. | Early. Good. Early is when things get done.
line @5 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. I'm locking up shortly, so be brief. | Evening. I've the bolts to do, and then I'm yours.
line @6 greet [pair.band=friends|family] : Sit down. There are oatcakes, and I buttered them myself. | Come in. I've put the kettle where it'll stay warm.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Home before the lamps. Good. I'd started counting the hours. | In with you. Both bolts, please.
line @1 again.open : A second visit today. I'll write it in the book. | Again. Something was forgotten, I expect.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : A word, before you pass. It's about the beans. | This way a moment. Two minutes, by the bell.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#. Say what's wanted. | #greet#. Well met.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : And to you. There's a stool, if you'll stop. | You came. Good. Sit down.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. I'm at the tally, but I can listen. | #greet#. Keep talking; I count better with a voice nearby.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Good evening. The gate lamps are going. | Evening. I'll be home within the hour.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Bright One go before you on the road. | #farewell#. Shut the door firmly; it sticks. | #farewell#.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Lamplit streets only, mind. | Good night. You'll be in my prayers before I sleep.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | And you. Safe home. | #bless#.
line @2 farewell.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Lock up behind you. | Good night. Bright One guard your door.
line @1 react.news : Say it plainly, please, and in order. | I'm listening. From the start, if you would.
line @1 react.news.new : None of that has come in at #gate#. I'd have had it. | New to me. I'll mark the day I heard it.
line @1 react.news.known : The carters had that yesterday. | Two customers told it me before nine.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Hearsay, that, until a witness owns to it. | That's one account. I prefer two.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : #rest#. I'll remember it at chapel. | A heavy thing. There will be a candle for it.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Good. I'll mark that in my heart and on the calendar both. | Thanks be. Something going right is worth marking.
line @1 answer.how : Well. The count came right this morning. | Well, thank you. Busy, which is better than idle.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Busy. Three carts unloaded and only the one day book. | Run off my paws. I'll be easier when the shelves are full.
line @1 answer.how.poorly : A little chesty. The damp. It's nothing. | Tired. It was a long count.
line @1 ask.how : And you. Keeping well? | How are things at home with you?
line @1 sympathise : Hard. Sit on the sack there; I'll make tea. | I'm sorry for it. You'll have a candle from me on Restday.
line @1 reassure : Patience and a little prayer. It mends more than you'd credit. | Give it a week. A week settles most accounts.
line @1 decline : I'd rather not. The store needs me; I'm promised to it till the bolts are drawn. | Not today. There's a delivery wants checking and nobody else to check it.
line @1 accept_decline : I understand. Another time. | Of course. Your answer would have been mine.
line @1 thank : Thank you. That was well done. | I'm obliged. It goes in the book, on the good side.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It needed doing, and I was there. | No thanks needed. You'd do the same.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes] : The shelves are calling me back. | The day book won't fill itself in.
line @2 preclose : Well. That's enough of your time spent. | Home for me, before the lamps.
line @1 accept_preclose : Yes, go. You've given me enough of the day. | Yes. We'll talk again.
line @1 ack : Noted. | That's so. | So it's written.
line @1 give.advice : Count it when it comes in, count it when it goes out. Then you'll sleep. | Keep your keys on one ring and know every one of them.
line @2 give.advice [other.stage=young|adolescent] : Learn your figures. Nobody can cheat a pup who can add. | Say your prayers and wipe your paws. In that order.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Robbers on the north road. Go with a guarded cart or not at all. | Don't travel alone this week. The carters are going in threes.
scene keys_in_the_dark
when topic=smalltalk|work
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I can find any key on #ring# with the lamp out. Each one has its own weight. | Nineteen keys. I know them the way you'd know your own teeth. | Somebody once hid the ring from me for a joke. The drawer gave it away; I heard it jingle.
b : Every one of them? | That's a lot of keys. | That's a poor joke.
a : Every one. The small brass one is the cellar. It's the lightest and the most important. | It's the right number. Each door has one and no door has two. | It was. I don't care for jokes about keys.

scene morning_count
when topic=work self.job=merchant here.time=dawn
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Before I lift the shutter I walk #shelves# and count. Every morning. | I count the rope first, then the sacks, then the oil. Always in that order. | The count is my first prayer each morning, and the only one I say on my feet.
b : Every morning? | Why that order? | You count the stock as a prayer?
a : Every morning since I took the keys. A day I skip is a day something goes missing. | Heavy things first. If they're short, the rest hardly matters. | In a manner of speaking. The Bright One likes things where they belong.

scene the_one_short
when topic=work self.job=merchant
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : One sack short this morning. One. I counted it four times. | The tally says forty lamps and the shelf says thirty-nine. I'll find the fortieth. | A gap on the third shelf, where last night there was none.
b : It'll turn up. | Perhaps it was sold. | Somebody moved it, maybe.
a : It did. Behind the door, where a carter left it. I'll speak to the carter. | Then it's in the day book, and I'll find it there. Things are always somewhere. | Perhaps. I'll look until I know. I won't sleep otherwise.

scene carts_in
when topic=caravan|work world.wagons=in here.region=ridgemere
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : Carts in. I heard the wheels at #gate# before the bells rang. | Four carts down the north road today, all four lighter than I ordered. | Wheels in the yard at first light. Shelves full by evening, Bright One willing.
b : That's good. | Lighter? | Full by evening?
a : It is. A full store is a quiet mind. | Lighter. Somebody between here and there is short of honesty. I'll find out who. | If the carters stop talking long enough to unload.

scene carts_late
when topic=caravan|times world.wagons=late here.region=ridgemere
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : Late, the carts, again. A shelf of empty hooks and a customer asking every hour. | Two days late, the north road carts. I've stopped writing a date in the book. | They're late, and nobody at #gate# will tell me why.
b : They'll come. | Two days isn't much. | Somebody will know.
a : They will. Which day is what I want, so I can stop listening for wheels. | It's two days of folk asking me and getting nothing. | Then that somebody should come and tell me. I'm not hard to find.

scene carts_robbed
when topic=caravan|bandits|crime world.wagons=raided
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : A cart's been stripped on the north road. My stock for the month with it. | Robbers again. A carter came in at #gate# with nothing behind but an empty bed. | They've taken a cart. Bright One shelter the carter; the goods can go.
b : That's terrible. | Was anyone hurt? | The goods can go?
a : It is. I'll pray for the carter and write the loss in red. | I wasn't told. Nobody tells a storekeeper that part. | They can. Rope comes again on the next cart. A carter doesn't.

scene customer_waiting
when topic=work self.working=yes
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Somebody stood at the counter a quarter hour this morning, deciding between two kinds of string. | I can't abide #waiting#. Hand me the job instead and it's done. | The factor said noon. It's past two. I've done three hours' work standing here.
b : String? | It's hard to wait. | Two hours late.
a : Two kinds, the same price. They bought neither. | It is. Each minute gets counted, and I wish I didn't. | And no word of why. I've written it down.

scene grayrock_roof
when topic=work self.job=merchant self.workplace
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : Whoever holds the deed to #self.workplace#, I'm the one who keeps it. That's a different thing. | A clerk comes down once a month to check my books. Not one mistake found yet. | Somebody else's stock, somebody else's roof. The count is mine.
b : Does that sit well with you? | Never a mistake? | Yours and nobody else's.
a : It does. I'd rather keep a store than own one. Owning is worry with a deed on it. | Never once. I'd be ashamed if a clerk found one. | Mine. If a nail's missing, I'm the one who answers.

scene gate_bar
when topic=day|smalltalk here.region=ridgemere here.time=dusk|night
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Listen. That's #gate# shutting. The bar goes down heavy. | Every night I hear the gate close and I check my own bolts after. | When the gate shuts, the carts that aren't in are out for the night. I pray for those.
b : All this way off? | Every night? | That's kind.
a : From here and from home. I've heard it all my life. | Every night. If the city locks up, so do I. | It costs me a breath. They carry my stock.

scene travellers_ask
when topic=newcomer|travel here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Travellers come in through #gate# and stop at my door first. I'm the first lamp they see. | A family, road-weary, asked me where to sleep. I pointed them to the chapel. | Folk new to the city ask me where things are. I can tell them to the shelf.
b : First lamp. I like that. | The chapel? | To the shelf?
a : It is. The road's news reaches my door an hour before the criers. | The chapel never turns anyone away. I'd do the same, if I had the room. | Within these walls I could. The city, near enough.

scene newcomer_coin
when topic=newcomer
does give.opinion / agree / assess
a : Strangers settling on the street. Good. Newcomers buy rope and lamp oil. | Somebody new took the rooms over the chandler's. I've already sold them a lamp. | A new neighbour pleases me. More so once the first bill's paid.
b : You're glad of it. | A lamp already? | A fair way to see it.
a : Glad, and plainly so. | And a broom. A sensible start. | A paid bill says more of a wolf than a smile does.

scene garden_hour
when topic=pastimes|home here.time=dusk|day
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : After I lock up I go home and dig for an hour. It empties my head of figures. | I turned over #beds# last night. The soil's heavy. I like heavy. | There's nothing like a spade for putting a day in its place.
b : What do you grow? | Heavy soil? | A spade?
a : Leeks, beans, and a row of onions I'm proud of. | Heavy holds the water. In this city that's not hard. | A spade. In, turn, lift. No arguing with it.

scene garden_rain
when topic=weather|pastimes here.weather=rain
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : No digging today. The beds are a pond. | The rain's at it again. My beans will be up to their knees. | I went out with #spade# and came back in. The ground wasn't having it.
b : It'll dry out. | Beans don't mind water. | Better to wait.
a : By Restday, perhaps. I'll wait, though I don't care to. | Some water. Not this much. | Better to wait, yes. I've never been good at it.

scene oatcakes_supper
when topic=food
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #oatcakes.capitalize#. Six nights of seven, and no wish for other. | I bake the oatcakes myself on Restday, enough for the week. | There's nothing wrong with a plain supper. An oatcake is honest food.
b[job=baker|cook] #food : Do you put salt in yours? | A week's worth? | Honest's the word.
b : Every night? | A week's worth? | Plain's best.
a@food : A pinch. Not more. Some folk drown them. | Twelve a day. I count them out. | It is. You can't hide anything in an oatcake.
a : Six of seven. Fuss at table never suited me. | Twelve a day. I count them out. | It is. A plain supper and a quiet house.

scene larder_full
when topic=home|food|season here.season=autumn
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : My larder's stocked to the lintel. I could feed the street till spring. | I've put by enough for the winter. Sleep comes easier for it. | Last night I opened the larder only to look. I'm not ashamed.
b : That's a comfort. | Enough for all winter? | Just to look?
a : It is. Each jar up there is a prayer answered. | And a little over, for whoever knocks. | Just to look. I counted the jars while I was there.

scene summer_light
when topic=season|weather here.season=summer here.time=dusk
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Here's the finest hour summer gives. #evening.capitalize#, and nothing to count. | I sat out till nearly ten last night. The light wouldn't go. | Evenings like this, I forgive the city its rain.
b : It's lovely. | Nearly ten? | All of it?
a : It is. I'll go in when it's gone, and not before. | Nearly. I had the spade out until the last of it. | Most of it. Ask me again in the autumn.

scene night_racket
when topic=day|smalltalk here.time=night here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : There's #racket# from the gate inns every night now. I lie awake counting the songs. | Carters singing below my window past midnight. I'll speak to the innkeeper. | Somebody rolled a barrel down our street at two. I heard every cobble.
b : Inns will be inns. | Will that help? | Every cobble?
a : They'll be quiet inns, if I have my say. | It will not. I'll speak anyway. | Each. Forty-one cobbles to the corner, I counted.

scene damp_in_sacks
when topic=weather|work here.weather=rain|fog self.job=merchant
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : The damp is in the sacks again. I've had them off the floor all morning. | Wet weather. The flour's been turned twice, and a third time's coming. | Fog's got into the store. Everything smells of the Sound.
b : You've a cough. | It'll dry. | Does it spoil the stock?
a : It's nothing. A tickle. The sacks matter more. | It will, if I'm stubborn. I am. | Not if they're turned. They'll be turned.

scene fog_at_gate
when topic=weather here.weather=fog here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : No #gate# to be seen from the store today, and I can always see the gate. | Fog. The carts will be crawling. I'll not expect them before noon. | They've lit the gate lamps at midday. That's how thick it is.
b : It's thick. | Crawling? | In the day?
a : Thick as wool. I'll keep the shutter half down. | Crawling, and the carters cursing. I don't blame them. | In the day. I lit my own as well.

scene restday_chapel
when topic=faith here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Same pew at chapel, Restday after Restday, since I was small. | Every Restday I give thanks for a full store and a dry roof. | One prayer for the carters, one for the count. Both get heard, I trust.
b[piety=devout] #devout : I sit near the back myself. | Plenty to give thanks for. | I pray for the carters too.
b : The same place? | That's a lot to give thanks for. | Both?
a@devout : Then we've likely nodded to each other. I'll look for you. | There is. Most folk forget to say so. | Then they've two of us. Good.
a : The same place. Third row, by the pillar. | It is. I try not to take it for granted. | Both. Choosing between them would be unkind.

scene child_late
when topic=family self.parent=yes
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : When #self.child# is out past the lamps, there's no sleep for me till the latch goes. | A lamp burns in my window for #self.child#. It always has. | I worry over #self.child# more than over the store. That's as it should be.
b : You'll hear the door. | That's kind. | As it should be.
a : I will. The latch always goes, in the end. | One candle a night. Cheap at the price. | It is. A store can be restocked.

scene neighbour_wall
when topic=friends|home self.neighbour
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #self.neighbour.capitalize# could hold one of my keys. Few could. | One wall between #self.neighbour# and me for years, and no quarrel through it. | A lit lamp next door means #self.neighbour# is home. I like knowing.
b : From you, that's praise. | Never once? | It's a comfort.
a : It is. I count my keys carefully. | Not once. We're both quiet, and that helps. | It is. A quiet street is a lit window and a shut door.

scene learner_count
when topic=work self.master=yes self.job=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I've a learner who can't count rope without losing their place. I've them do it again. | I teach the count first. Before the prices, before the customers. | The learner asked me why I count twice. Once is guessing, I said.
b : Again? | Before the prices? | Once is a guess.
a : Again and again, until it's right. Then once more. | Prices change. The count doesn't. | It is. Twice is a fact. I'd like the learner to know the difference.

scene prices_written
when topic=prices self.job=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Every price goes on a card, pinned to its shelf. Nobody need ask. | Lamp oil's gone up again. New figure on the card, old figure underneath. | I don't haggle. The card says it, and the card is honest.
b : That's fair. | The old one under it? | Never haggle?
a : Fair to me, and just as fair to them. | So they can see what it was. I'd want to know, in their place. | Never. A price you can talk down was never the price.

scene hard_times
when topic=times|prices world.times=hard|lean
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : Folk are buying half measures this month. Half a rope. I've never sold half a rope. | Things are sitting on the shelves. Things sitting is how a store goes quiet. | I've had three people ask to pay next week. I've said yes to all three. I wrote it down.
b : It'll turn. | It's a hard time. | That's good of you.
a : It will. Until it does I'll cut rope. | It is. My prayers go to the shelves, and to the folk who'd buy if they could. | It's written down. Kindness and a book can go together.

scene good_times
when topic=times|work world.times=good|boom self.job=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : The shelves empty faster than the carts can fill them. That's a good year. | Twice this month I've sent to the wharf for more rope. Twice. | Everybody's buying. I can't keep the count up with the selling.
b : That's good, isn't it? | More rope? | A good problem.
a : It is. Thanks on Restday, and a fresh count the morning after. | More rope. The city must be tying itself together. | A happy trouble. I'll bear it.

scene gossip_exact
when topic=gossip topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted|prowling|violence|other
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : I heard #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's how it was said to me, word for word. | Across my counter it came that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I add nothing to what I hear. | A carter told me #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've not repeated it until now.
b : Is that so? | You don't add to it? | Why tell me?
a : It's what I heard. I'll not make it bigger. | Never. A story grows enough without help. | Because you asked, and because you'll keep it to yourself.

scene gossip_wedding
when topic=gossip|event topic.claim_kind=marriage|promise_kept
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : Good news across the counter today: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. | I heard #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'm glad. That's a prayer of thanks for tonight. | There's a happy thing going round: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#.
b : That's lovely. | A prayer? | Happy, yes.
a : It is. We need more of it. | A prayer of thanks. They're the easy ones. | Happy. I'll be glad all day.

scene thieves_about
when topic=crime world.crime=recent here.region=ridgemere
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : There's been thieving on the street. I checked #bolts# three times last night. | Somebody's been light-pawed on the Commons. The till sits at the back now. | A shop was broken into by the gate. I've asked the Watch to come by twice of a night.
b : The Watch will be about. | That's wise. | Twice?
a : They will. I'll check my bolts regardless. | It's only sense. I'd rather move a till than lose one. | Twice. I'll count them going past.

scene winter_road
when topic=season|caravan here.season=winter
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : The north road's half shut with the snow. I've stocked for it, but I don't like it. | In winter the carts come once a week if the road's kind. I order three weeks ahead. | Snow at the gate. The carters will want hot drinks and I'll give them tea.
b : You're ready for it. | Three weeks ahead? | Tea?
a : I'm ready. I'm not glad. | Three weeks. One empty winter taught me. | Tea. I'll not keep anything stronger.

scene market_morning
when topic=day here.day=market here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Market day. Half of Northgate tramps by my door to the square. | On market days I open an hour early. Folk want rope before they buy the thing to tie up. | It's market day, and I've already sold more twine than all of last week.
b : Busy, then. | An hour early? | Twine?
a : Busy. I'll eat standing up. | An hour. It's worth it. | Twine. Everybody has something to tie on market day.

scene walled_garden
when topic=dreams|pastimes
does confide / react.self / assess
a : I'd like a garden with a wall round it. Not big. Just mine, and quiet. | Someday, a garden with a gate I can lock. My beds now are borrowed ground. | Some coin goes aside each month for a walled garden. I've the place in mind.
b[friends] #friends : You've wanted that for years. | You'll have it. | Where?
b : A walled garden? | A gate you can lock? | Where is it?
a@friends : I have. I'm patient. I'm also stubborn. | I will. The sum's worked out already. | Not saying. It's not mine yet.
a : A wall, a gate, and a key on my ring that's for nothing but the garden. | Of course. I'd want to know it was mine. | Not saying. It's not mine yet.

story first_keys [topic=work|memories self.age>=35 self.job=merchant]
  : The day they gave me the keys, I sat on a crate in the back aisle and learnt them one by one.
  : It took me till dark. I didn't go home until I could find every lock with my eyes shut.
  ask : Have you ever been handed something you weren't sure you could keep?
  b : Now and then. Were you ready for it?
  : Not at all. I felt ready by the morning, which was soon enough.

story the_night_count [topic=work self.job=merchant]
  : One night the count was out by a single coil of rope, and I couldn't sleep.
  : I got up, dressed, and walked down to the store with a lantern to count it again.
  : It was there. It had been under a sack all along.
  b : All that for one coil? Was it worth it?
  : It was worth the sleep I had after. I'd do it again.

story the_long_winter [topic=season|memories self.age>=40]
  : One winter the north road shut for six weeks, and no cart came through the gate.
  : We sold what we had down to the last nail. Folk brought back things they didn't need, so others could have them.
  ask : Would you have brought back what you'd bought, for a neighbour?
  b : I'd want to. Whether I would, I can't promise.
  : Most did. I've thought better of this street ever since.

story the_honest_coin [topic=newcomer|memories self.age>=30]
  : A traveller once came back through the gate a week after buying, to pay me a copper I'd undercharged.
  : I'd not noticed. I'd counted wrong. That traveller had come a day out of the way to put it right.
  b : That's rare. Did you thank them?
  : I did. I wrote the copper in the day book with a mark beside it. It's still there.

story first_onions [topic=pastimes|memories]
  : The first year I dug the beds, I planted onions too close together and got nothing but leaves.
  : The second year I measured every row with a length of string. Every one came up.
  ask : Do you measure things, or do you trust your eye?
  b : My eye, mostly. It's wrong often enough.
  : Mine was too. I trust the string now.

rule @bark_keys
when concept=bark
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Nineteen. All there. | Where's the small brass one. There.

rule @bark_count
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.job=merchant
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Forty, forty-one, forty-two. | Twice counted. Good.

rule @bark_carts
when concept=bark world.wagons=late
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Still no carts. | Bright One bring them in before dark.

rule @bark_gate
when concept=bark here.time=dusk here.region=ridgemere
does aside
cooldown 12h
say There goes the gate. | Bar's down. Bolts next.

rule @bark_racket
when concept=bark here.time=night
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Will they never stop singing. | There's a store to open at dawn, you know.

rule @bark_damp
when concept=bark here.weather=rain|fog
does aside
cooldown 9h
say The sacks will want turning. | It's nothing. Only a tickle.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. You'll want something, I expect. Name it, and I'll say if it's on a shelf. | #greet#. Mind the sacks, they're counted.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. I remember you. You paid. | #topic.player#. Good. What do you need?
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : It's past the lamps. I'm only here to check the bolts. | It's late. Be quick, please; the house is waiting.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Your business, please. I'll hear it from there. | The counter stays between us, I think.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=merchant] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Rope, lamps, sacks, oil and nails, counted twice. | I keep #self.workplace#. The deed's another's; the count is mine.
dlg @2 resident.job : I work, I count the work, and I sleep at night. There's my trade entire. | My work is keeping things where they belong. It's more work than it sounds.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : The shelves empty quicker than the carts fill them, thanks be. | Thriving. I've sent for more twice this month.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. The count comes right and the rent's paid. | Steady, thank you. Asking for more would be greed.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Poorly. Things sit on the shelves. I'm praying and I'm counting. | Not well. I'll not pretend otherwise.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : It came through the gate from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. I've told it as I had it.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : Every customer today has had #world.event_words# to tell me. Twelve, so far.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Robbers stripped a cart on the north road. Wait for a guarded one if you're going out.
dlg @4 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# The carts came and went, and the count came right. | Not a scrap new in at the gate today.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : I heard #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's as it was said; I've not added to it.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : I don't carry tales. I carry rope. | Nothing I'd repeat, and nothing I'd want repeated.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Bright One see you safe. | #farewell#. Shut the door firmly; it sticks.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Lamplit ways home, please. | Good night. I've the bolts to see to.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop=open] : #greet#. Rope, lamps, sacks, oil and nails. Prices on the cards. | #greet#. Name it and I'll fetch it. Everything here is counted.
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop=open topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. The usual, or something fresh? | #topic.player#. Your custom's welcome. What do you need?
dlg @1 shop.prices : Every price is on its card. No haggling here, and no cheating either. | Every price is written down. If it's gone up, the old one's under it.
dlg @1 shop.bye : Thank you. That's your change, counted twice over. | #farewell#. When you run low, you know the door.
dlg @1 master.learn [here.teaches=yes] : I'll teach the count first. If you can't count, you can't keep. #learn_offer# | Keeping a store is knowing where everything is, every day. #learn_offer#
dlg @2 master.learn [here.teaches=yes topic.meeting=known] : You've come back. Good. Tell me what's on the third shelf. | Back again. Let's see if you remember the order.
graft resident.hub
choice That's a heavy ring of keys. -> keys
node keys
say Nineteen keys, and each one known by its weight. The cellar key I could find in a black cellar with my eyes shut.
mood calm
free
choice How do you tell them apart? -> keys_how
choice Something else. -> hub
node keys_how
say Each one's a different weight in the paw. The small brass one is lightest, and it's the one that matters most. You learn them, or you lose doors.
choice I see. Something else. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft master.learn
choice How do you keep a count true? -> count
node count
say Every coil coming in gets counted. Every coil going out, the same. Both in the book. Then at dawn, see if the shelf agrees with it.
mood formal
free
choice And if it doesn't? -> count_wrong
choice Back to the lesson. -> learn
node count_wrong
say Then you count again, and again, until it does. You don't go home with a wrong count. Not once.
choice Back to the lesson. -> learn
choice Goodbye. -> bye
